From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kiran Patchala <pkiran@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:43:56 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] net/ixgbe: add non-coherent DMA Rx/Tx burst functions

Add non-coherent (NC) scalar burst functions for ARM64 platforms where
the PCIe DMA path is not hardware-coherent (e.g. Qualcomm SoCs).

Cache maintenance:
  - CPU->Device (flush):  DC CVAC + DSB ISH.
  - Device->CPU (invalidate): DC CIVAC + DSB ISH.
    DC CIVAC used instead of DC IVAC because DC IVAC is EL1-privileged
    and causes SIGILL in userspace (EL0).

Enabled via devarg non_coherent=1. Vector paths are disabled.

Additional fixes:
  - New RX mbuf buffer invalidated before DMA handoff to NIC.
  - rte_io_wmb() added before qrx_tail doorbell write.
  - txp double-flush eliminated with txp_flushed flag.
  - rte_packet_prefetch() moved before intel_cache_invalidate() in RX.
  - Redundant rte_atomic_thread_fence(acquire) removed after invalidate.
  - All debug printf() replaced with PMD_TX_LOG(DEBUG, ...).

Signed-off-by: Kiran Patchala <pkiran@qti.qualcomm.com>

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Yocto specific]
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/intel/common/intel_cache_ops.h b/drivers/net/intel/common/intel_cache_ops.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0000001
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/net/intel/common/intel_cache_ops.h
@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
+ * Copyright(c) 2026 Intel Corporation
+ */
+
+#ifndef _INTEL_CACHE_OPS_H_
+#define _INTEL_CACHE_OPS_H_
+
+#include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <rte_common.h>
+
+/*
+ * Cache maintenance helpers for non-coherent DMA systems.
+ *
+ * Mirrors the Linux kernel dma_map_single()/dma_unmap_single() semantics
+ * as implemented in arch/arm64/mm/cache.S and arch/arm64/mm/dma-mapping.c:
+ *
+ *  intel_cache_flush()      - CPU wrote, device will DMA-read (map equivalent)
+ *                             Uses DC CVAC (clean to PoC) per cache line +
+ *                             DSB ISH. Writes CPU cache back to memory so the
+ *                             NIC DMA engine sees the data. No invalidation
+ *                             needed (CPU cache remains valid). DC CVAC on
+ *                             partial end lines is safe: clean does not evict,
+ *                             no adjacent data is lost.
+ *                             Use for: Tx packet data, Tx descriptors,
+ *                             Tx context descriptors, Rx refill descriptors.
+ *
+ *  intel_cache_invalidate() - Device DMA-wrote, CPU will read (unmap equivalent)
+ *                             Uses DC CIVAC (clean+invalidate to PoC) for ALL
+ *                             cache lines + DSB ISH. DC IVAC is EL1-only and
+ *                             causes SIGILL in userspace (EL0). DC CIVAC is
+ *                             permitted from EL0 and is functionally correct:
+ *                             the clean part is a no-op when the CPU has not
+ *                             written the line (NIC wrote it), so there is no
+ *                             correctness difference vs DC IVAC for DMA receive.
+ *                             Single DSB ISH after the loop.
+ *                             Use for: Tx descriptor writeback DD poll,
+ *                             Rx descriptor writeback DD poll, Rx packet data,
+ *                             new Rx mbuf buffer pre-handoff to NIC.
+ *
+ * On all other architectures (x86, etc.): no-ops because those platforms
+ * guarantee DMA coherency in hardware.
+ *
+ * Note: ARM64 requires DMA buffers to be ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN (128-byte) aligned.
+ * Callers must ensure mbuf data buffers satisfy this alignment.
+ */
+
+#ifdef RTE_ARCH_ARM64
+
+static __rte_always_inline void
+intel_cache_flush(void *addr, size_t len)
+{
+	uintptr_t a = (uintptr_t)addr & ~((uintptr_t)RTE_CACHE_LINE_SIZE - 1);
+	const uintptr_t end = (uintptr_t)addr + len;
+	for (; a < end; a += RTE_CACHE_LINE_SIZE)
+		asm volatile("dc cvac, %0" :: "r"(a) : "memory");
+	asm volatile("dsb ish" ::: "memory");
+}
+
+/*
+ * intel_cache_invalidate - invalidate a memory region before CPU reads
+ * NIC-DMA-written data.
+ *
+ * Uses DC CIVAC (clean+invalidate to PoC) for all lines. DC IVAC would be
+ * more precise (invalidate only, no clean needed since CPU didn't write these
+ * lines) but DC IVAC is EL1-privileged and causes SIGILL in userspace (EL0).
+ * DC CIVAC is permitted from EL0 (ARMv8 ARM D7.4.6) and the extra clean is
+ * a no-op in practice since the CPU has not written NIC-DMA'd lines.
+ */
+static __rte_always_inline void
+intel_cache_invalidate(const void *addr, size_t len)
+{
+	uintptr_t a = (uintptr_t)addr & ~((uintptr_t)RTE_CACHE_LINE_SIZE - 1);
+	const uintptr_t end = (uintptr_t)addr + len;
+	for (; a < end; a += RTE_CACHE_LINE_SIZE)
+		asm volatile("dc civac, %0" :: "r"(a) : "memory");
+	asm volatile("dsb ish" ::: "memory");
+}
+
+#else /* !RTE_ARCH_ARM64 */
+
+#define intel_cache_flush(addr, len)      RTE_SET_USED(addr)
+#define intel_cache_invalidate(addr, len) RTE_SET_USED(addr)
+
+#endif /* RTE_ARCH_ARM64 */
+
+#endif /* _INTEL_CACHE_OPS_H_ */

